-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party material


This software contains material derived from two projects by Evan Czaplicki,
both licensed under the same BSD 3-Clause License reproduced above. Their
copyright notices are retained here, as clause 1 requires, and apply to the
files listed under each.

elm/core -- https://github.com/elm/core
Copyright 2014-present Evan Czaplicki

    lib/prelude/Basics.elm
    lib/prelude/Char.elm
    lib/prelude/Maybe.elm
    lib/prelude/String.elm
    lib/prelude/Tuple.elm
        Module and value names, type signatures and bodies taken from the
        elm/core modules of the same names; a subset of the originals.

    lib/codegen/js/dartea_runtime.js
        Rewritten in modern JavaScript from Elm/Kernel/Utils.js, Basics.js and
        Char.js, keeping the original algorithms and their constants.

    lib/data/kernel.ml
        The set of Elm.Kernel.* primitives a prelude module may refer to, and
        their arities -- the kernel surface of elm/core, as a closed sum.

    lib/prelude/prelude.ml
        The list of prelude modules and the default imports every module
        receives, following Elm/Compiler/Imports.hs of elm/compiler.

These files are embedded into the compiler binary and are compiled into
Basics.mjs, Char.mjs, Maybe.mjs, String.mjs, Tuple.mjs and Dartea_runtime.mjs
alongside every program this compiler builds. Redistributing that output
redistributes material derived from elm/core.

elm/compiler -- https://github.com/elm/compiler
Copyright 2012-present Evan Czaplicki

Text reproduced verbatim:

    lib/reporting/report.ml
        Diagnostic wording taken word for word from Reporting/Error/Type.hs,
        Canonicalize.hs, Syntax.hs, Import.hs and Pattern.hs, including the
        hint texts and the elm-lang.org documentation links.

    test/errors/*.txt
        Golden files containing that wording.

Structure mirrored rather than text copied:

    lib/reporting/category.ml
    lib/reporting/context.ml
    lib/reporting/expectation.ml
    lib/reporting/hint.ml
    lib/reporting/type_error.ml
    lib/reporting/name_error.ml
    lib/reporting/syntax_error.ml
    lib/reporting/warning.ml
        The taxonomy of errors -- the Category, Context, Expected and hint
        sums of Reporting/Error/Type.hs and the per-phase error sums --
        translated into OCaml. These files contain no English text.

    lib/reporting/doc.ml
    lib/reporting/snippet.ml
        Report layout: the document combinators of Reporting/Doc.hs and the
        numbered source excerpt with the ^^^ underline of
        Reporting/Render/Code.hs.

The Elm language itself -- its syntax, its operator precedences and the names
and signatures of its standard library -- is followed deliberately, because
compatibility is the point of this project. That is a specification, not
copied expression, and it is named here for honesty rather than as a licence
obligation.

This software is an independent implementation. It is not affiliated with or
endorsed by the Elm project or by Evan Czaplicki.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Studied, not copied


No code from these is present here. They are named because they shaped design
decisions and because the reader deserves to know where the ideas came from.

    ReScript and Melange
        Runtime data representation (string tags, TAG and _0 fields, cons
        cells), the uncurried calling convention, module-qualified access,
        and the rule that a backend builds AST nodes instead of splicing
        JavaScript text.

    Luc Maranget, Compiling Pattern Matching to Good Decision Trees (ACM
    ML'08) and Warnings for Pattern Matching (JFP 2007)
        The decision-tree compilation and usefulness algorithms in
        lib/after_typed/exhaustive.ml.

    Alex Reinking, Ningning Xie, Leonardo de Moura and Daan Leijen, Perceus:
    Garbage Free Reference Counting with Reuse (PLDI 2021)
        The reference-counting scheme of lib/codegen/c/dartea_runtime.h. The
        dup / drop / reuse vocabulary is the paper's. The object layout was
        chosen here. This file was not compared line by line against the Koka
        runtime; no evidence of copied code was found, but that is an
        inspection, not a proof.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Linked dependencies


The compiled binary links the following libraries. Their own licenses apply to
them and their notices are preserved by their respective distributions.

    menhirLib        LGPL-2.0-only WITH OCaml-LGPL-linking-exception
    OCaml runtime    LGPL-2.1-or-later WITH OCaml-LGPL-linking-exception
    base             MIT
    non_empty_list   MIT
    ppx_hash         MIT
    eio, eio_main    ISC
    containers-data  BSD-2-Clause

menhirLib and the OCaml runtime are LGPL. The OCaml linking exception permits
linking them into a binary under these terms without that binary becoming
subject to the LGPL.

The menhir parser generator itself is GPL-2.0-only. It is a build-time tool and
is not linked into the binary, in the same way a compiler is not.

Used only to build and test, never linked into the shipped binary:
ppx_jane (MIT), ppx_deriving (MIT), ounit2 (MIT), qcheck-core and qcheck-ounit
(BSD-2-Clause).
